e evaluate how well  or poorly an economy is doing we  understand that the stock market is a  component but it is merely one of many  components we should be looking at we  must look at the employment rate  and unemployment rate we have to look at  wage growth we have to look at the  quality of jobs we must look at  inflation and inequality and really you  want to look at quality of life factors  as well what is life expectancy  educational factors where is literacy  right there are so many ways to figure  out like how is a country doing a big  picture but let's for a second grant  them that there is an empirical reality  stock markets either do better under  Democratic presidential administrations  or Republican presidential  administrations we can measure it we can  know this there are a whole bunch of  different ways to do it so you've got to  make some decisions like I'll give you  some examples do you would just stock  market for a performance for inflation  or not when comparing Democratic and  Republican performance do you calculate  returns with dividends reinvested or not  okay so there's like little tweaks in  terms of how you calculate it no matter  how you do it every serious analysis  finds that stocks do better under  Democratic presidents than they do under  Republican presidents if you go back to  1929 on average stocks make about ten  percent a year during democratic  presidencies and about two percent a  year during Republican presidencies  that's one study from Forbes another  study that looked only at the last 45  years or so found that the stock market  return is about 14 percent per year  under Democratic presidents 10 percent  per year under Republican presidents now  the difficulty of these numbers is that  you can have sort of outlier years that  can skew the averages but from an  empirical historical perspective the  stock market does better when there's a  Democrat in the White House instead of a  Republican but that's looking back ok we  